                    "reply_count": 0,
                    "retweet_count": 0,
                    "source": "Twitter Web App",
                    "text": "That can be reduced by getting rid of foreigners who Americans are subsidizing through the ACA.  The ACA is also used as an income redististribution scheme, a.k.a., welfare, with millions paying nothing, including migrants/non Americans, transferring money from working Americans to foreigners.\n\nIndividuals at or below 130% of the Federal Poverty Level (FPL) typically pay little to nothing in monthly premiums, often a $0 monthly premium for a benchmark Silver plan or even a Gold-like plan (due to cost-sharing reductions).\n\nHere is a breakdown of the price range after subsidies and tax credits, based on current (2025) guidelines:\nPremium Tax Credits (APTC): The Affordable Care Act (ACA) provides enhanced premium tax credits (subsidies) to make coverage affordable. Under current law (as of November 2025), individuals and families with incomes between 100% and 150% of the FPL are eligible for fully subsidized benchmark plans, meaning their expected contribution is 0% of their income for the benchmark Silver plan.\n\nCost-Sharing Reductions (CSRs): In addition to premium subsidies, people in this income bracket who enroll in a Silver plan also qualify for significant cost-sharing reductions. These subsidies reduce their out-of-pocket costs (deductibles, copayments, and out-of-pocket maximums) to levels similar to Gold or Platinum plans, effectively providing a high-value plan for little or no premium.\n\nAverage Cost: The resulting average net premium paid by these enrollees is usually in the range of $0 to a few dollars per month. For example, a single individual making $22,000 (around 140% FPL) might see their monthly premium rise from $0 to around $66 if the enhanced tax credits expire (which is not the case for the 2025 plan year), illustrating how effective the current subsidies are at eliminating premiums for this income group.\n\nThe exact price can vary slightly based on the specific plan chosen (if they opt for a plan more expensive than the benchmark), age, and location, but the subsidy ensures the cost of the benchmark plan is zero or near-zero.\n\nNon US citizens can participate in the ACA, receiving both subsidies and tax credits (direct cash payments for people who haven't even paid taxes), all at the expense  of Americans. These people must be removed from eligibility. Green card holders, refugees, asylees, and Cuban/Haitian entrants are some examples of foreigners being subsidized out of the pockets of Americans. Get them off and then fund it for Americans only.\n\nThis is bullshit. This is how Democrats fucked Americans to pay for foreigners. Our country is 38 TRILLION in debt and any such programs need to be exclusively for Americans, which will reduce the costs of the ACA.",
